'''Palestinian Baliscans''' ({{Langx|bl|Baliscanos palestinos}}; {{langx|ar|فلسطينيون باليسكيون|Filasṭīniyyūn Bālīskiyyūn}}) are Baliscan citizens of full or partial [[w:Palestinians|Palestinian ancestry]]. According to the [[2025 Baliscan census]], they number 3,106,584, constituting 2.01% of the total population of the [[Baliscan Confederation]].

This community forms a significant segment of the broader Levantine Baliscan community, which itself is part of the larger [[West Asian and North African Baliscans|West Asian and North African]] (WANA Baliscans) population, which current to 2025 as well, numbered over 28 million people. The Palestinian community in Balisca is notable for being the largest populations of Palestinians outside the [[W:Arab world|Arab world]], characterized by deep historical roots dating back to the mid-19th century and a high degree of socioeconomic integration.

== Migration history ==
The history of Palestinian migration to Balisca is characterized by several distinct waves, driven by economic opportunities and, later, geopolitical conflict in the Levant.

The earliest Palestinian migrants arrived in Balisca in the 1850s, in the face of the [[W:Crimean War|Crimean War]], in which the [[W:Russian Empire|Russian Empire]] aimed to seize the [[W:Holy Land|Holy Land]] from the the [[W:Ottoman Empire|Ottoman Empire]]. This first wave consisted primarily of Christian merchants and artisans from towns such as [[W:Bethlehem|Bethlehem]], [[W:Jerusalem|Jerusalem]], and [[W:Ramallah|Ramallah]], who were fleeing the Ottoman policy of mandatory military service for Christians. In response, the Baliscan government established immigration offices throughout the territory of the Ottoman Empire (alongside other countries in Afro-Eurasia), with the goal of attracting some of the millions of people migrating to the Americas to the Baliscan archipelago. 

These early migrants would establish early dominance in import and export networks in Balisca, importing Levantine textiles, [[W:Olive oil|olive oil]], and crafts, laying the foundation for future social mobility within their communities. 

The collapse of the Ottoman Empire and the hardships of [[W:World War I|World War I]] triggered a second, larger wave of migration. Many Palestinians, fearing conscription and famine, fled the Levant. Balisca, with its sizable existing Palestinian community and growing industrial economy, became a favored destination.

=== Palestinian ''Nakba'' and post-1948 migration ===
{{Main|w:Nakba|w:Naksa}}
The pivotal event dramatically shaping the Palestinian Baliscan community was the [[W:1948 Arab-Israeli War|1948 Arab-Israeli War]], which Palestinians call the [[W:Nakba|Nakba]] ({{langx|ar|النَّكْبَة||the catastrophe }}). While Balisca had already established itself as a major destination for Palestinians seeking political and economic stability, the Nakba fundamentally shifted the nature of the migration from voluntary economic movement to forced mass displacement. Following the establishment of Israel and the subsequent flight and expulsion of over 700,000 Palestinians from their homes, Balisca became one of the primary non-Arab-world destinations for refugees. Many who arrived in Balisca during this period had lost all assets and relied heavily on the existing, well-integrated Palestinian Baliscan community for resettlement aid. This influx included a greater proportion of rural dwellers and people from the coastal cities who previously had less representation, broadening the community's geographic and socioeconomic diversity significantly.

The migration trend accelerated following the [[W:1967 Six-Day War|1967 Six-Day War]] and Israel's subsequent occupation of the [[W:West Bank|West Bank]], [[W:Gaza Strip|Gaza Strip]], and [[W:East Jerusalem|East Jerusalem]]. This conflict led to another major wave of displacement, often referred to as the [[W:Naksa|Naksa]] ({{langx|ar|النكسة||the setback}}). The heightened political instability, severe limitations on movement and economic activity in the occupied territories, and the inability of displaced persons to return drove thousands more to seek permanent residency in Balisca. Unlike previous waves, many of the post-1967 arrivals included professionals, educators, and civil servants who found opportunities limited under occupation, leading to an intellectual and professional enrichment of the diaspora.

=== Contemporary ===
In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, migration from the Palestinian territories and the wider Palestinian diaspora (e.g., from refugee camps or other Arab states experiencing conflict) has continued, though at a lower, more steady rate driven by persistent economic hardship and political uncertainty. The overwhelming majority of Palestinian Baliscans are now second, third, and fourth-generation citizens, characterized by a high degree of integration. They maintain a strong bicultural identity, speaking Baliscano fluently while preserving Arabic language, culinary traditions, and cultural connections to Palestine. The community has been actively involved in cultural preservation and transnational civil society efforts related to the [[W:Palestinian nationalism|Palestinian]] [[W:Palestinian nationalism|struggle for liberation]], primarily through cultural organizations, academic initiatives within Balisca, and support for Palestinian resistance.

